IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `coa5-derivative-claims`

-- coa-proc-derivative-claims.l4: Companies Act 2006, Part 11, Chapter 1, ss 260 to 264 (derivative claims in England and Wales or Northern Ireland): the procedure the Act lays down
-- when a member applies for permission (in Northern Ireland, leave) to continue a derivative claim, written as regulative rules over the Boolean encoding in
-- coa5-derivative-claims.l4. Lines 3379-3465 of ukpga-2006-46.txt. Chapter 2 (ss 265 to 269, Scotland) is not encoded here.
-- The gates are the existing functions of coa5-derivative-claims.l4 (whether the member must apply, whether s 262 or s 264 applies and the grounds are made out, whether the court
-- must dismiss, whether it may give directions, which courses it has on a hearing, and whether s 263(2) requires permission to be refused). Nothing here edits that module,
-- and nothing here calls its DEONTIC for s 261(1).
--
-- THE TEXT STATES NO TIME LIMIT anywhere in ss 261 to 264: no period for the application, none for the court's dismissal, directions, adjournment or hearing, and none
-- for the company's evidence (the court's directions fix that). So no rule below has a WITHIN, and none has a LEST that a clock would reach: a duty or a power that has not
-- been used stays open in the trace (RESIDUAL) however long the trace waits. The text states no consequence of not applying or of a court's inaction either.
--
-- SEAMS taken as INPUTS: what the court is satisfied of (that the application and the evidence disclose no prima facie case, that a person acting in accordance with s 172 would
-- not seek to continue the claim, that the act or omission was authorised or has been ratified); that the court is hearing the application; the facts of the grounds
-- (abuse of process, failure to prosecute diligently, that it is appropriate for the member to continue). Each court step is its own rule with its own day 0, because the court's
-- finding at that step is an input (the same reason as in coa-proc-register-copy.l4, which measured that the court's alternative outcomes written as MAY events in a ROR do not
-- commit to the branch whose event happened).

DECLARE `ss 260-264 party` IS ONE OF
    `ss 260-264 the member who brings the derivative claim`
    `ss 260-264 the member who applies to continue the company's claim`
    `ss 260-264 another member of the company`
    `ss 260-264 the court`

DECLARE `ss 260-264 act` IS ONE OF
    `s 261(1) the member applies to the court for permission (in Northern Ireland, leave) to continue the derivative claim`
    `s 261(2)(a) the court dismisses the application`
    `s 261(2)(b) the court makes a consequential order`
    `s 261(3)(a) the court gives directions as to the evidence to be provided by the company`
    `s 261(3)(b) the court adjourns the proceedings to enable the evidence to be obtained`
    `s 261(4)(a) the court gives permission (or leave) to continue the claim on such terms as it thinks fit`
    `s 261(4)(b) the court refuses permission (or leave) and dismisses the claim`
    `s 261(4)(c) the court adjourns the proceedings on the application and gives such directions as it thinks fit`
    `s 262(2) the member applies to the court for permission (in Northern Ireland, leave) to continue the claim as a derivative claim`
    `s 262(3)(a) the court dismisses the application`
    `s 262(3)(b) the court makes a consequential order`
    `s 262(4)(a) the court gives directions as to the evidence to be provided by the company`
    `s 262(4)(b) the court adjourns the proceedings to enable the evidence to be obtained`
    `s 262(5)(a) the court gives permission (or leave) to continue the claim as a derivative claim on such terms as it thinks fit`
    `s 262(5)(b) the court refuses permission (or leave) and dismisses the application`
    `s 262(5)(c) the court adjourns the proceedings on the application and gives such directions as it thinks fit`
    `s 264(2) another member applies to the court for permission (in Northern Ireland, leave) to continue the claim`
    `s 264(3)(a) the court dismisses the application`
    `s 264(3)(b) the court makes a consequential order`
    `s 264(4)(a) the court gives directions as to the evidence to be provided by the company`
    `s 264(4)(b) the court adjourns the proceedings to enable the evidence to be obtained`
    `s 264(5)(a) the court gives permission (or leave) to continue the claim on such terms as it thinks fit`
    `s 264(5)(b) the court refuses permission (or leave) and dismisses the application`
    `s 264(5)(c) the court adjourns the proceedings on the application and gives such directions as it thinks fit`

-- ---- s 263(1) and (2): when permission (or leave) must be refused -------------------------------------------------------------

-- src:3424 | 263	Whether permission to be given
-- src:3425 | (1) The following provisions have effect where a member of a company applies for permission (in Northern Ireland, leave) under section 261 or 262.
-- src:3426 | (2) Permission (or leave) must be refused if the court is satisfied—
-- src:3427 | (a) that a person acting in accordance with section 172 (duty to promote the success of the company) would not seek to continue the claim, or
-- src:3428 | (b) where the cause of action arises from an act or omission that is yet to occur, that the act or omission has been authorised by the company, or
-- src:3429 | (c) where the cause of action arises from an act or omission that has already occurred, that the act or omission—
-- src:3430 | (i) was authorised by the company before it occurred, or
-- src:3431 | (ii) has been ratified by the company since it occurred.
@ref s 263(1)-(2) Whether permission to be given: when it must be refused (src:3424-3431)
-- FORK: s 263(1) "under section 261 or 262" | s 263 has effect on an application under s 261 or s 262 and, on the words of s 263(1), not on another member's application under s 264, so the refusal below is encoded for ss 261 and 262 only | s 263 governs the s 264 application as well
GIVEN `the act or omission is yet to occur or has already occurred` IS A `s 263 when the act or omission occurs`
      `the court is satisfied that a person acting in accordance with section 172 (duty to promote the success of the company) would not seek to continue the claim` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court is satisfied that the act or omission has been authorised by the company (before it occurs, where it is yet to occur)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court is satisfied that the act or omission was authorised by the company before it occurred` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court is satisfied that the act or omission has been ratified by the company since it occurred` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 263(1)-(2) permission (or leave) must be refused on an application under section 261 or 262` MEANS
        (`s 263(1) the provisions of this section have effect` TRUE) EQUALS JUST TRUE
    AND (`s 263(2) permission (or leave) must be refused` `the act or omission is yet to occur or has already occurred` `the court is satisfied that a person acting in accordance with section 172 (duty to promote the success of the company) would not seek to continue the claim` `the court is satisfied that the act or omission has been authorised by the company (before it occurs, where it is yet to occur)` `the court is satisfied that the act or omission was authorised by the company before it occurred` `the court is satisfied that the act or omission has been ratified by the company since it occurred`) EQUALS JUST TRUE


-- ======================================================================================================================
-- s 261
-- ======================================================================================================================

-- ---- s 261: the application for permission (or leave) --------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- src:3394 | 261	Application for permission to continue derivative claim
-- src:3395 | (1) A member of a company who brings a derivative claim under this Chapter must apply to the court for permission (in Northern Ireland, leave) to continue it.
@ref s 261(1) Application for permission to continue derivative claim: the member must apply (src:3394-3395)
-- TIMELINE: unit=none; day 0=the day the member brings the derivative claim; counting=silent (s 261(1) says "must apply" and gives no time and no consequence, so no clock is encoded and the application stays owed until the trace shows it)
-- FORK: s 261(1) "must apply" | a duty of the member with no clock and no breach clause: the text states neither a time nor a consequence, so the rule has no WITHIN and no LEST | the missed application is a breach (the existing rule for s 261(1) says so, but its LEST can never be reached without a deadline)
GIVEN `a member of a company brings a derivative claim under this Chapter` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC `ss 260-264 party` `ss 260-264 act`
`s 261(1) the member's step of applying for permission to continue the derivative claim` MEANS
    IF (`s 261(1) the member must apply to the court for permission to continue the claim` `a member of a company brings a derivative claim under this Chapter`) EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN PARTY `ss 260-264 the member who brings the derivative claim`
         MUST `s 261(1) the member applies to the court for permission (in Northern Ireland, leave) to continue the derivative claim`
         HENCE FULFILLED
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- ---- s 261(2): the court dismisses an application that discloses no prima facie case, and may make a consequential order ------

-- src:3396 | (2) If it appears to the court that the application and the evidence filed by the applicant in support of it do not disclose a prima facie case for giving permission (or leave), the court—
-- src:3397 | (a) must dismiss the application, and
-- src:3398 | (b) may make any consequential order it considers appropriate.
@ref s 261(2) Dismissal where no prima facie case, and any consequential order (src:3396-3398)
-- TIMELINE: unit=none; day 0=the day it appears to the court that the application and the evidence disclose no prima facie case; counting=silent (s 261(2) says "must dismiss" and gives no time, so no clock is encoded and the duty stays owed until the trace shows it done)
-- FORK: s 261(2) "it appears to the court" | the court's view is an input to the rule, and the duty to dismiss arises when the input is true | the duty arises only once the court has stated its view in a decision
GIVEN `the application and the evidence disclose no prima facie case for giving permission (or leave)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC `ss 260-264 party` `ss 260-264 act`
`s 261(2)(a) the court's step of dismissing an application that discloses no prima facie case` MEANS
    IF (`s 261(2) the court must dismiss the application` `the application and the evidence disclose no prima facie case for giving permission (or leave)`) EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN PARTY `ss 260-264 the court`
         MUST `s 261(2)(a) the court dismisses the application`
         HENCE FULFILLED
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- TIMELINE: unit=none; day 0=the day the court dismisses the application; counting=silent (s 261(2)(b) says "may make any consequential order it considers appropriate" and gives no time, so no clock is encoded and the power stays open until the trace shows it used)
-- FORK: s 261(2)(b) "may make any consequential order" | a separate power of the court, open only where the court must dismiss, and not a condition of the dismissal being complete | the consequential order is the next step of the dismissal, so a dismissal with no order is unfinished
GIVEN `the application and the evidence disclose no prima facie case for giving permission (or leave)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC `ss 260-264 party` `ss 260-264 act`
`s 261(2)(b) the court's power to make a consequential order after a dismissal` MEANS
    IF (`s 261(2)(b) the court may make any consequential order it considers appropriate` (`s 261(2) the court must dismiss the application` `the application and the evidence disclose no prima facie case for giving permission (or leave)`)) EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN PARTY `ss 260-264 the court`
         MAY `s 261(2)(b) the court makes a consequential order`
         HENCE FULFILLED
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- ---- s 261(3): directions as to the evidence, and adjournment to obtain it -----------------------------------------------------

-- src:3399 | (3) If the application is not dismissed under subsection (2), the court—
-- src:3400 | (a) may give directions as to the evidence to be provided by the company, and
-- src:3401 | (b) may adjourn the proceedings to enable the evidence to be obtained.
@ref s 261(3) Directions as to the evidence, and adjournment (src:3399-3401)
-- TIMELINE: unit=none; day 0=the day the court decides not to dismiss the application; counting=silent (s 261(3)(a) says "may give directions" and gives no time, so no clock is encoded)
-- FORK: s 261(3)(a) "directions as to the evidence to be provided by the company" | only the court's power to give the direction is encoded: the text lays no duty on the company and no time for its evidence, which the court's own directions set | the company's duty to provide the evidence is a rule of this section
GIVEN `the application and the evidence disclose no prima facie case for giving permission (or leave)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC `ss 260-264 party` `ss 260-264 act`
`s 261(3)(a) the court's power to give directions as to the evidence to be provided by the company` MEANS
    IF (`s 261(3) the court may give directions as to the evidence and may adjourn the proceedings` (`s 261(2) the court must dismiss the application` `the application and the evidence disclose no prima facie case for giving permission (or leave)`)) EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN PARTY `ss 260-264 the court`
         MAY `s 261(3)(a) the court gives directions as to the evidence to be provided by the company`
         HENCE FULFILLED
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- TIMELINE: unit=none; day 0=the day the court decides not to dismiss the application; counting=silent (s 261(3)(b) says "may adjourn the proceedings" and gives no time, so no clock is encoded)
-- FORK: s 261(3) (a) and (b) | two separate powers, each its own rule, so that using one does not finish the other | one rule offering both
GIVEN `the application and the evidence disclose no prima facie case for giving permission (or leave)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC `ss 260-264 party` `ss 260-264 act`
`s 261(3)(b) the court's power to adjourn the proceedings to enable the evidence to be obtained` MEANS
    IF (`s 261(3) the court may give directions as to the evidence and may adjourn the proceedings` (`s 261(2) the court must dismiss the application` `the application and the evidence disclose no prima facie case for giving permission (or leave)`)) EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN PARTY `ss 260-264 the court`
         MAY `s 261(3)(b) the court adjourns the proceedings to enable the evidence to be obtained`
         HENCE FULFILLED
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- ---- s 261(4): on hearing the application ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- src:3402 | (4) On hearing the application, the court may—
-- src:3403 | (a) give permission (or leave) to continue the claim on such terms as it thinks fit,
-- src:3404 | (b) refuse permission (or leave) and dismiss the claim, or
-- src:3405 | (c) adjourn the proceedings on the application and give such directions as it thinks fit.
@ref s 261(4) On hearing the application (src:3402-3405)
-- helper: the court is hearing the application and each of the three courses of the text is open to it (the existing function answers for one course at a time)
GIVEN `the court is hearing the application` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 261(4) the court is hearing the application and each of its three courses is open` MEANS
        (`s 261(4) the court's course on hearing the application` (`s 261(4)(a) give permission (or leave) to continue the claim on such terms as it thinks fit`) `the court is hearing the application`) EQUALS JUST TRUE
    AND (`s 261(4) the court's course on hearing the application` (`s 261(4)(b) refuse permission (or leave) and dismiss the claim`) `the court is hearing the application`) EQUALS JUST TRUE
    AND (`s 261(4) the court's course on hearing the application` (`s 261(4)(c) adjourn the proceedings on the application and give such directions as it thinks fit`) `the court is hearing the application`) EQUALS JUST TRUE

-- TIMELINE: unit=none; day 0=the day the court hears the application; counting=silent (s 261(4) says "On hearing the application, the court may" and gives no time, so no clock is encoded; an adjournment under (c) is followed by a fresh run of this rule from the day the hearing resumes)
-- FORK: s 263(2) "must be refused" | where the court is satisfied of a matter in s 263(2) the only step the rule offers is the refusal of s 261(4)(b), as a duty (MUST), and giving permission is barred by the separate rule after this one; adjourning is not offered once the court is satisfied | the court may still adjourn under s 261(4)(c) and refuse later
-- FORK: s 261(4)(a) "on such terms as it thinks fit" | the terms are not modelled: giving permission is one act | each term is a further step
GIVEN `the court is hearing the application` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the act or omission is yet to occur or has already occurred` IS A `s 263 when the act or omission occurs`
      `the court is satisfied that a person acting in accordance with section 172 (duty to promote the success of the company) would not seek to continue the claim` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court is satisfied that the act or omission has been authorised by the company (before it occurs, where it is yet to occur)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court is satisfied that the act or omission was authorised by the company before it occurred` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court is satisfied that the act or omission has been ratified by the company since it occurred` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC `ss 260-264 party` `ss 260-264 act`
`s 261(4) the court's step on hearing the application` MEANS
    IF (`s 261(4) the court is hearing the application and each of its three courses is open` `the court is hearing the application`)
    THEN (IF `s 263(1)-(2) permission (or leave) must be refused on an application under section 261 or 262` `the act or omission is yet to occur or has already occurred` `the court is satisfied that a person acting in accordance with section 172 (duty to promote the success of the company) would not seek to continue the claim` `the court is satisfied that the act or omission has been authorised by the company (before it occurs, where it is yet to occur)` `the court is satisfied that the act or omission was authorised by the company before it occurred` `the court is satisfied that the act or omission has been ratified by the company since it occurred`
          THEN (PARTY `ss 260-264 the court` MUST `s 261(4)(b) the court refuses permission (or leave) and dismisses the claim` HENCE FULFILLED)
          ELSE (   (PARTY `ss 260-264 the court` MAY `s 261(4)(a) the court gives permission (or leave) to continue the claim on such terms as it thinks fit` HENCE FULFILLED)
                ROR (PARTY `ss 260-264 the court` MAY `s 261(4)(b) the court refuses permission (or leave) and dismisses the claim` HENCE FULFILLED)
                ROR (PARTY `ss 260-264 the court` MAY `s 261(4)(c) the court adjourns the proceedings on the application and gives such directions as it thinks fit` HENCE FULFILLED)))
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- src:3424 | 263	Whether permission to be given
-- src:3425 | (1) The following provisions have effect where a member of a company applies for permission (in Northern Ireland, leave) under section 261 or 262.
-- src:3426 | (2) Permission (or leave) must be refused if the court is satisfied—
-- src:3427 | (a) that a person acting in accordance with section 172 (duty to promote the success of the company) would not seek to continue the claim, or
-- src:3428 | (b) where the cause of action arises from an act or omission that is yet to occur, that the act or omission has been authorised by the company, or
-- src:3429 | (c) where the cause of action arises from an act or omission that has already occurred, that the act or omission—
-- src:3430 | (i) was authorised by the company before it occurred, or
-- src:3431 | (ii) has been ratified by the company since it occurred.
@ref s 263(2) Permission (or leave) must be refused: the bar on giving it (src:3424-3431)
-- TIMELINE: unit=none; day 0=the day the court hears the application; counting=silent (s 263(2) says "must be refused" and gives no time, so no clock is encoded: the bar is never lifted by waiting, and the trace stays open until the court gives permission, which is the breach)
-- FORK: s 263(2) "must be refused" | the bar is its own rule (SHANT give permission), beside the duty to refuse in the rule above, because a prohibition with no clock never completes and would keep a RAND of the two open after the court had refused | one rule, the duty and the bar together
GIVEN `the court is hearing the application` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the act or omission is yet to occur or has already occurred` IS A `s 263 when the act or omission occurs`
      `the court is satisfied that a person acting in accordance with section 172 (duty to promote the success of the company) would not seek to continue the claim` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court is satisfied that the act or omission has been authorised by the company (before it occurs, where it is yet to occur)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court is satisfied that the act or omission was authorised by the company before it occurred` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court is satisfied that the act or omission has been ratified by the company since it occurred` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC `ss 260-264 party` `ss 260-264 act`
`s 263(2) the court's bar on giving permission (or leave) on an application under section 261` MEANS
    IF (`s 261(4) the court is hearing the application and each of its three courses is open` `the court is hearing the application`) AND `s 263(1)-(2) permission (or leave) must be refused on an application under section 261 or 262` `the act or omission is yet to occur or has already occurred` `the court is satisfied that a person acting in accordance with section 172 (duty to promote the success of the company) would not seek to continue the claim` `the court is satisfied that the act or omission has been authorised by the company (before it occurs, where it is yet to occur)` `the court is satisfied that the act or omission was authorised by the company before it occurred` `the court is satisfied that the act or omission has been ratified by the company since it occurred`
    THEN PARTY `ss 260-264 the court`
         SHANT `s 261(4)(a) the court gives permission (or leave) to continue the claim on such terms as it thinks fit`
         HENCE FULFILLED
         LEST BREACH BY `ss 260-264 the court` BECAUSE "the court gave permission (or leave) although it was satisfied of a matter that requires permission (or leave) to be refused: s 263(2), on an application under s 261"
    ELSE FULFILLED

