-- ---- s 262(5): on hearing the application ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- src:3420 | (5) On hearing the application, the court may—
-- src:3421 | (a) give permission (or leave) to continue the claim as a derivative claim on such terms as it thinks fit,
-- src:3422 | (b) refuse permission (or leave) and dismiss the application, or
-- src:3423 | (c) adjourn the proceedings on the application and give such directions as it thinks fit.
@ref s 262(5) On hearing the application (src:3420-3423)
-- helper: the court is hearing the application and each of the three courses of the text is open to it (the existing function answers for one course at a time)
GIVEN `the court is hearing the application` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 262(5) the court is hearing the application and each of its three courses is open` MEANS
        (`s 262(5) the court's course on hearing the application` (`s 262(5)(a) give permission (or leave) to continue the claim as a derivative claim on such terms as it thinks fit`) `the court is hearing the application`) EQUALS JUST TRUE
    AND (`s 262(5) the court's course on hearing the application` (`s 262(5)(b) refuse permission (or leave) and dismiss the application`) `the court is hearing the application`) EQUALS JUST TRUE
    AND (`s 262(5) the court's course on hearing the application` (`s 262(5)(c) adjourn the proceedings on the application and give such directions as it thinks fit`) `the court is hearing the application`) EQUALS JUST TRUE

-- TIMELINE: unit=none; day 0=the day the court hears the application; counting=silent (s 262(5) says "On hearing the application, the court may" and gives no time, so no clock is encoded; an adjournment under (c) is followed by a fresh run of this rule from the day the hearing resumes)
-- FORK: s 263(2) "must be refused" | where the court is satisfied of a matter in s 263(2) the only step the rule offers is the refusal of s 262(5)(b), as a duty (MUST), and giving permission is barred by the separate rule after this one; adjourning is not offered once the court is satisfied | the court may still adjourn under s 262(5)(c) and refuse later
-- FORK: s 262(5)(a) "on such terms as it thinks fit" | the terms are not modelled: giving permission is one act | each term is a further step
GIVEN `the court is hearing the application` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the act or omission is yet to occur or has already occurred` IS A `s 263 when the act or omission occurs`
      `the court is satisfied that a person acting in accordance with section 172 (duty to promote the success of the company) would not seek to continue the claim` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court is satisfied that the act or omission has been authorised by the company (before it occurs, where it is yet to occur)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court is satisfied that the act or omission was authorised by the company before it occurred` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court is satisfied that the act or omission has been ratified by the company since it occurred` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC `ss 260-264 party` `ss 260-264 act`
`s 262(5) the court's step on hearing the application` MEANS
    IF (`s 262(5) the court is hearing the application and each of its three courses is open` `the court is hearing the application`)
    THEN (IF `s 263(1)-(2) permission (or leave) must be refused on an application under section 261 or 262` `the act or omission is yet to occur or has already occurred` `the court is satisfied that a person acting in accordance with section 172 (duty to promote the success of the company) would not seek to continue the claim` `the court is satisfied that the act or omission has been authorised by the company (before it occurs, where it is yet to occur)` `the court is satisfied that the act or omission was authorised by the company before it occurred` `the court is satisfied that the act or omission has been ratified by the company since it occurred`
          THEN (PARTY `ss 260-264 the court` MUST `s 262(5)(b) the court refuses permission (or leave) and dismisses the application` HENCE FULFILLED)
          ELSE (   (PARTY `ss 260-264 the court` MAY `s 262(5)(a) the court gives permission (or leave) to continue the claim as a derivative claim on such terms as it thinks fit` HENCE FULFILLED)
                ROR (PARTY `ss 260-264 the court` MAY `s 262(5)(b) the court refuses permission (or leave) and dismisses the application` HENCE FULFILLED)
                ROR (PARTY `ss 260-264 the court` MAY `s 262(5)(c) the court adjourns the proceedings on the application and gives such directions as it thinks fit` HENCE FULFILLED)))
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- src:3424 | 263	Whether permission to be given
-- src:3425 | (1) The following provisions have effect where a member of a company applies for permission (in Northern Ireland, leave) under section 261 or 262.
-- src:3426 | (2) Permission (or leave) must be refused if the court is satisfied—
-- src:3427 | (a) that a person acting in accordance with section 172 (duty to promote the success of the company) would not seek to continue the claim, or
-- src:3428 | (b) where the cause of action arises from an act or omission that is yet to occur, that the act or omission has been authorised by the company, or
-- src:3429 | (c) where the cause of action arises from an act or omission that has already occurred, that the act or omission—
-- src:3430 | (i) was authorised by the company before it occurred, or
-- src:3431 | (ii) has been ratified by the company since it occurred.
@ref s 263(2) Permission (or leave) must be refused: the bar on giving it (src:3424-3431)
-- TIMELINE: unit=none; day 0=the day the court hears the application; counting=silent (s 263(2) says "must be refused" and gives no time, so no clock is encoded: the bar is never lifted by waiting, and the trace stays open until the court gives permission, which is the breach)
-- FORK: s 263(2) "must be refused" | the bar is its own rule (SHANT give permission), beside the duty to refuse in the rule above, because a prohibition with no clock never completes and would keep a RAND of the two open after the court had refused | one rule, the duty and the bar together
GIVEN `the court is hearing the application` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the act or omission is yet to occur or has already occurred` IS A `s 263 when the act or omission occurs`
      `the court is satisfied that a person acting in accordance with section 172 (duty to promote the success of the company) would not seek to continue the claim` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court is satisfied that the act or omission has been authorised by the company (before it occurs, where it is yet to occur)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court is satisfied that the act or omission was authorised by the company before it occurred` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court is satisfied that the act or omission has been ratified by the company since it occurred` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC `ss 260-264 party` `ss 260-264 act`
`s 263(2) the court's bar on giving permission (or leave) on an application under section 262` MEANS
    IF (`s 262(5) the court is hearing the application and each of its three courses is open` `the court is hearing the application`) AND `s 263(1)-(2) permission (or leave) must be refused on an application under section 261 or 262` `the act or omission is yet to occur or has already occurred` `the court is satisfied that a person acting in accordance with section 172 (duty to promote the success of the company) would not seek to continue the claim` `the court is satisfied that the act or omission has been authorised by the company (before it occurs, where it is yet to occur)` `the court is satisfied that the act or omission was authorised by the company before it occurred` `the court is satisfied that the act or omission has been ratified by the company since it occurred`
    THEN PARTY `ss 260-264 the court`
         SHANT `s 262(5)(a) the court gives permission (or leave) to continue the claim as a derivative claim on such terms as it thinks fit`
         HENCE FULFILLED
         LEST BREACH BY `ss 260-264 the court` BECAUSE "the court gave permission (or leave) although it was satisfied of a matter that requires permission (or leave) to be refused: s 263(2), on an application under s 262"
    ELSE FULFILLED

